em is a classical graph theory problem which is NP-complete. ... We then characterize the SDP solutions upon joining two triangle graphs by an edge~sum.\n\n# Optimal solutions and ranks in the max-cut SDP\n\n`2109.02238` · 2021-09-06 · Cited by 1 · 🧠 ready\n\nDaniel Hong, Hyunwoo Lee, Alex Wei\n\nmath.OC\n\nThe max-cut problem is a classical graph theory problem which is NP-complete. The best polynomial time approximation scheme relies on semidefinite programming (SDP). We study the conditions under which graphs of certain classes have rank~1 solutions to the max-cut SDP. We apply these findings to look at how solutions to the max-cut SDP behave under simple combinatorial constructions. Our results determine when solutions to the max-cut SDP for cycle graphs are rank~1. We find the solutions to the max-cut SDP of the vertex~sum of two graphs. We then characterize the SDP solutions upon joining two triangle graphs by an edge~sum.\n\n📄 PDF on arXiv.org 🧠 Refresh Paper ◎ Find Similar [Input] Auto-prepare ↗ arxiv.org\n\nOriginal on arxiv.org PDF (external)\n\n\n## References\n\n## Cited By\n\n  * 2310.14983 Causal clustering: design of cluster experiments under network interference (Oct 2023) 4 cites\n\n## Source Files\n\n📁 Source files are served from arXiv.org for compliance--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFirst letters of permutations with a fixed inversion number | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/research/kagey-2021-inversion-first-letter-conjecture-5-1/)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago;   * Source identification, literature reading: Kagey Conjecture 5.1, arXiv:2112.05281v1, Section 5.2, page 20; external tier 1, preregistration #19726. ...   * Sibling, source identification: Conjecture 5.2 concerns the major index and has separate preregistration #19720; this inversion theorem does not settle it.\n\n# First letters of permutations with a fixed inversion number\n\nLiterature status: not rechecked Repository record: proved\n\nResolution source: D5/S3/Combinatorics/Permutation/KageyInversionFirstLetter.result10.48550/arXiv.2112.05281Source dossierRelease 36a49830636b\n\nProblem\n\n## The question\n\nPeter Kagey, “Expected value of letters of permutations with a given number of k-cycles”, arXiv:2112.05281v1, Section 5.2, page 20, Conjecture 5.1 states:\n\n> For fixed k and n > k, the expected value of the first letter of a permutation with a given number of inversions satisfies a rational function in n given by E[π(1) | π ∈ S_n, inv(π) = k] = M(n + 1, k)/M(n, k), where M(n, k), as above, is the number of permutations w ∈ S_n such that inv(w) = k.\n\n### Statement echo\n\n| Source clause | Formal counterpart | Fidelity | | --- | --- | --- | | Fixed $k$ and $n>k$ | `claim` universally quantifies both natural parameters and assumes $k<n$. | Exact. | | Permutations in $S_n$ | `Equiv.Perm (Fin n)` and its finite universal set. | Each permutation occurs once. | | One-based first letter | `PSW.word`, defined by the existing owner as the list of values plus one; `List.headD 0` selects its first entry. | $k<n$ excludes the empty permutation. | | Inversion number $k$ | `ListInversions.inv` applied to `PSW.word`. | Strict inverted pairs. | | $M(n,k)$ | `M`, cardinality of the inversion fiber. | Literal source cardinality. | | Conditional expectation | `E`, rational first-letter sum divided by rational cardinality. | Literal sum-over-cardinality convention. | | Ratio $M(n+1,k)/M(n,k)$ | The conclusion of `claim` and `result`. | Both casts and the strict guard are retained. |\n\nRational division is total in Lean. The theorem proves the ratio identity directly; its statement does not include a separate positivity assertion for $M(n,k)$.\n\n## Our foothold\n\nPreregistration #19726 identifies the external tier-1 conjecture and its literal conventions. The settling GID is `D5/S3/Combinatorics/Permutation/KageyInversionFirstLetter.result`, with admission basis `open-problem-resolution (#19726; Proved)`. Its Scribe carries one `OpenProblemResolutionClaim` with resolution `Proved`.\n\n## Missing bridges\n\nThe source and the inspected dissertation state this assertion as a conjecture. Finite enumeration alone cannot settle its universal quantifiers. The inspected later sources and repository search supplied no settlement in their searched scope; literature completeness is `ASSUMED-UNVERIFIED`.\n\nRoute\n\n## Proposed approach\n\nThe order-preserving first-letter decomposition removes exactly the rank of the first letter from the inversion count. Applying this decomposition twice gives two bounded inversion digits. Under $k<n$, a diagonal with total contribution $t$ has $t+1$ admissible digit pairs. Finite sum interchange therefore identifies the natural first-letter weighted numerator with $M(n+1,k)$. Casting this numerator identity to the rationals proves the equality without cancelling a denominator.\n\n## What would falsify this route\n\nA pair of natural numbers $k<n$ whose exact rational conditional first-letter mean differs from $M(n+1,k)/M(n,k)$ would refute the source claim. A failed inversion-insertion equation or guarded diagonal count would refute the corresponding proof route.\n\n## Evidence to collect\n\nThe module is `D5/S3/Combinatorics/Permutation/KageyInversionFirstLetter.lean`. Its sole public theorem `D5.S3.Combinatorics.Permutation.KageyInversionFirstLetter.result` proves `claim` for all natural $k<n$. The public definitions are `M`, `E`, and `claim`. The module reuses `ListInversions.inv`, `PSW.word`, and `Finset.expect_eq_sum_div_card` on the proof path.\n\nThe four-slot escape audit for `result` is unfinished: #20067. There is no compiled registration establishing the required source-family reconstruction, readout correspondence, observational dependence and independent interventions; no `declared_validated` status is asserted.\n\n### Experiment\n\nExact finite enumeration is stored in the experiments repository. Command: `python3 docs/reports/kagey-2021-first-letter-expectation-conjectures-5-1-5-2/check.py`, run from that repository, exit 0, `RESULT PASS`. The script SHA-256 is `d9a4f92ebf3ce0c7391a2632fd166c4f11b3c8890368268df7665e7708b58beb`. Its Conjecture 5.1 checks cover exactly $0\\le k<n\\le8$, enumerating permutations through size nine to obtain the next Mahonian counts. These finite readings support the universal theorem and do not replace its proof.\n\n## Scope assessment\n\n  * Proved, kernel-checked: the exact binding claim for every natural $k<n$, by `KageyInversionFirstLetter.result`.\n  * Source identification, literature reading: Kagey Conjecture 5.1, arXiv:2112.05281v1, Section 5.2, page 20; external tier 1, preregistration #19726.\n  * Mechanism, kernel-checked: the private `inv_decompose` gives the first-letter inversion contribution; `weighted_identity` gives the universal natural numerator identity used by `result`.\n  * Computed: the pinned experiment checks the ratio for exactly $0\\le k<n\\le8$; its command, exit code and script identity are given above.\n  * Boundary, proved in prose: for $n=k=3$, the only permutation with three inversions is $321$, so the first-letter mean is $3$. The six permutations of size four with three inversions are $1432$, $2341$, $2413$, $3142$, $3214$, and $4123$. Thus $M(4,3)/M(3,3)=6$, and the ratio identity fails without the strict guard. This finite boundary is not a separate Lean theorem.\n  * Sibling, source identification: Conjecture 5.2 concerns the major index and has separate preregistration #19720; this inversion theorem does not settle it.\n  * Open: a separately stated denominator-positivity theorem, extensions beyond $k<n$, and exhaustive literature screening. The required integration consists of the frozen settling module, its Scribe and this dossier; the literature boundary remains explicit.\n\n### What the settlement shows\n\nThe decisive identity is the weighted numerator, established by the private `weighted_identity` and used in the public `result`. It covers all $k<n$, including the length-one case, with the original conventions. Two successive insertions count the same objects as the first-letter weight because their inversion digits have an untruncated diagonal under the strict guard. The boundary above shows that removing the guard does not preserve the identity. The source's major-index question is a separate statement; no consequence for its settlement is claimed here.\n\n### Declaration classification\n\nThe sole public theorem `result` has `proof_shape: content`; its surviving intermediate witness is the private `weighted_identity`, whose derivation uses `inv_decompose`. The module's admission basis is `open-problem-resolution (#19726; Proved)`; `generality: I`, `utility: none`. Its internal length-one decision is an induction base, not a standalone computational certificate.\n\n| Declaration | `proof_shape` | `escape_witness` | Direct consumers | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `result` | content | `weighted_identity` | settling conclusion | | `E_eq_expect` | bind-only | none | `result` | | `M_decompose` | content | `inv_decompose` | `double_decompose` | | `count_ofFn` | bind-only | none | `inv_decompose`, `inv_ofFn_congr` | | `double_decompose` | content | `inv_decompose` | `weighted_identity` | | `inv_decompose` | content | `inv_ofFn_congr` | `weighted_decompose`, `double_decompose`, `M_decompose` | | `inv_ofFn_congr` | content | `inv_ofFn_congr` | `inv_decompose` | | `rectangle_count` | bind-only | none | `weighted_identity` | | `sum_digit` | bind-only | none | `rectangle_count` | | `sum_unit_digit` | bind-only | none | `rectangle_count` | | `weighted_decompose` | content | `inv_decompose` | `weighted_identity` | | `weighted_identity` | content | `inv_decompose` | `result` |\n\nASSUMED-UNVERIFIED\n\n## Unverified assumptions\n\nLiterature completeness is `ASSUMED-UNVERIFIED`. The supplied source screen covers arXiv v1, the author's dissertation, 