 balancing the comparison errors against the 1/r gap; it need not describe the actual growth rate.\n\nThere is a more specific prediction suggested by the Gaussian infinite-component spin glass. Hastings [11] derived a ground-state dimension of order \\(n^{2/5}\\). Aspelmeier and Moore [1] supported this scaling numerically and gave an alternative argument balancing a finite-dimension energy correction of order \\(1/r\\) against a spectral correction of order \\((r/n)^{2/3}\\). Balancing these two terms gives \\(r\\) of order \\(n^{2/5}\\). Motivated by this prediction, we propose the following statement for unweighted random graphs.\n\n**Conjecture 6.2.** If \\(G\\sim G(n,\\frac12)\\), then\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log \\rho(G)}{\\log n}\n\\xrightarrow{\\mathbb{P}}\n\\frac{2}{5}.\n\\]\n\nThe spin-glass calculations motivate this conjecture but do not establish it for the graph model. In particular, agreement of leading optimal values does not imply agreement of exact optimal ranks. Programming **62** (1993), 557–574.\n\n7. Marianna Eisenberg-Nagy, Monique Laurent, and Antonios Varvitsiotis, *Forbidden minor characterizations for low-rank optimal solutions to semidefinite programs over the elliptope*, J. Combin. Theory Ser. B **108** (2014), 40–80.\n\n8. Chris Godsil, David E. Roberson, Brendan Rooney, Robert Šámal, and Antonios Varvitsiotis, *Universal completability, least eigenvalue frameworks, and vector colorings*, Discrete Comput. Geom. **58** (2017), no. 2, 265–292.\n\n9. Michel X. Goemans and David P. Williamson, *Improved approximation algorithms for maximum cut and satisfiability problems using semidefinite programming*, J. ACM **42** (1995), 1115–1145.\n\n10. Robert Grone, Stephen Pierce, and William Watkins, *Extremal correlation matrices*, Linear Algebra Appl. **134** (1990), 63–70.\n\n11. Matthew B. Hastings, *Ground state and spin glass phase of the large N infinite range spin glass via supersymmetry*, J. Stat. Phys. **99** (2000), 171–198.\n\n12. Daniel Hong